# Chapter 9, first part: what an equation is, editing, place, screen, print.

.h2 What an equation is [eq-what]
.ix Equation marker, type 9
.ix Marker type 9
.ix Equation text
.ix Alt-F12
Marker type 9, the Equation marker, is a marker that holds text, as the Note and
the Comment markers do.  An equation is one item of the text: it can be
selected, copied, cut, pasted, moved and deleted like a letter, and it counts
as one position of the line.  How a marker is inserted and what the others
are is described in {x:markers} and in {x:edit-b-marker}; this
chapter is about the Equation marker.

The text of the equation is TeX math notation.  LaTeX, with the packages
amsmath and amssymb, typesets it.  Three consequences are true
all through the chapter.
- Every command of math mode of LaTeX and of amsmath and amssymb is available,
  not only the ones that this chapter lists; the tables list those that were
  tried and typeset with the result shown.
- A mistake in the text is found by TeX: the message is the message of TeX
  ({n:eq-errors}).
- The text of an equation is not changed by loading and saving.

.table 17 43
What is needed | For what
{c:latex} and {c:dvipng} | The pictures on the screen (X11 version).  Both are looked
 for in the system directories ({n:appcmd-find}) when the Editor starts.  Without them every
 equation keeps its label.
LaTeX with amsmath and amssymb | Printing and preview.  The prerequisites are
 the same as for the printing of any document ({n:appcmd}, Programs).
The X11 version | Only the X11 version draws pictures.  The terminal version
 shows labels at all times.
.end

.h2 Inserting and editing an equation [eq-insert]
.ix Insert an equation
.ix Edit an equation
.ix Equation Edit
.ix EQUATION EDIT
.ix Marker Commands, Equation
.ix Ctrl-F12
.ix Ctrl-W, view an equation
.ix Equation, view
.ix Ins saves, Esc restores, Ctrl-W views the equation

.h3 Insert [eq-insert-new]
Put the cursor where the equation goes and press {k:Alt-F12} (or {k:Ctrl-F12},
which Strata offers because many terminals and window managers keep
{k:Alt-F12} for themselves).  The box Marker Commands opens, with the names of
the markers in three columns.  Equation is the tenth name in the first column,
after Endif.  Move to it with the arrow keys or by typing {k:E} (the first
letter takes the selection to the next name that begins with E: Else, Endif,
Equation; press it until Equation is highlighted) and press {k:Ins} or
{k:Return}.  The editor of the equation opens at once.
.pic pictures/equations-grid.png 5.5 Marker Commands with Equation selected.
The command is not on the {k:F12} menu bar.  The terminal version reaches it through
{k:Ctrl-F} and the menu of the Alt commands (see {n:edit-b-marker}).
The marker can be placed in structured and unstructured text, in a table
column, in notes and comments.

.h3 The editor of the equation [eq-editor]
.ix Equation Edit editor
.ix Restore previous version
The editor of the equation is a full editor of the kind that edits a note: the
text of the equation is edited with the same keys as the text of the document.
The status line shows the mode EQUATION EDIT at the right, the bottom line
shows "Ins saves, Esc restores, Ctrl-W views the equation", and the name field
shows (new) for a new equation.
.pic pictures/equations-edit.png 5.5 The editor of an equation (X11).
.table 8 52
Key | Effect
{k:Ins} | Keep the text and go back to the document.  The marker is put in (a
 new one) or changed (an existing one) and the document is marked as changed
 (the star in the status line).  The key counts only without Shift, Ctrl or Alt
 and when no block is highlighted.
{k:Esc} | Throw the changes away and go back to the document as it was.  Not
 taken while a block is highlighted: it then cancels the highlight.
{k:Ctrl-W} | View the equation as it stands now ({n:eq-view}).
.end
All the other keys are those of the text editor: cursor keys, delete, copy
and paste of the function keys.  What you type is the text of the equation.
The text is read as follows:
- The lines are joined.  {k:Return} starts a new line in the editor, so that a
  long formula can be written in parts; every end of line counts as a space,
  and the label of the marker shows the lines on one line.  A space has no
  effect on the result in math mode.
- The text of an equation has no attributes: bold, underline and italic that
  you give to the characters, and tab characters, are dropped.  Use
  {c:\mathbf} and the other commands of {x:eq-fonts} for bold and the others.
- The blocks that follow the first one are read as further lines.  TeX
  comments ({c:%}) are refused when printing.
.ix Equation, lines joined
.ix Equation, attributes dropped

.h3 Edit an equation that is in the text [eq-insert-edit]
Put the cursor on the marker, so that the status line shows the marker (in the
terminal version the label is highlighted; in the X11 version the cursor is a
box round the whole picture) and press {k:Alt-F12} or {k:Ctrl-F12}.  The editor
opens with the text.  When the cursor is not on the marker but after it, the
same key opens the table Marker Commands instead and a new marker would be
inserted.  The panels of the other markers show fields; the Equation marker
has none, and its editor opens directly.
.pic pictures/equations-cursor.png 5.5 The cursor on a displayed equation.

.h3 Other things done to an equation [eq-insert-other]
.ix Copy an equation
.ix Delete an equation
.ix Move an equation
An equation is an item of the text.  Delete and Backspace remove it like any
character.  Cut, Copy, Paste and Move ({k:F3}, {k:F4}, {k:F5}, {k:F7}) work on it as on
any marker, together with the text around it; a block that holds an equation
carries it along.  Search does not look inside the text of an equation.  The
count of the words of the document does not count what a marker holds
({n:edit-b-search}).

.h2 Inline and displayed equations [eq-place]
.ix Displayed equation
.ix Inline equation
.ix On its own
.ix Display style
.ix Text style
Strata does not store whether an equation is displayed (set on a line of its
own, centered, at full size) or inline (set in the line of text, at text size).
Each time the line is laid out, the place of the marker determines it, by this
rule.
.table 24 36
Place of the marker | The equation is
The only item of its line (besides spaces, tabs and attribute changes) |
 Displayed.
There is other text or a character on the line, before or after it | Inline.
Another marker is on the line | Inline (a marker counts as something else on
 the line).
Two equations on one line | Both inline.
An equation followed by a full stop or a comma | Inline: the character after it
 is other text.
An equation in a column of a table (a block of several columns) | Judged for
 its column: alone in the column it is displayed on the screen.
.end
A line is the text between two ends of line of a column, or from the start or
to the end of the column.  A block of one column that holds
one equation and nothing else is therefore a displayed equation; two
equations on two lines of one block are two displayed equations.  The rule
is evaluated when the page is laid out: typing a letter next to a displayed
equation makes it inline at once, and removing it makes it displayed again.
There is no way to have a displayed equation followed by punctuation:
leave the punctuation out, or put it into the text of the equation
(for instance {c:x=1.}).
.pic pictures/equations-screen.png 5.5 Inline and displayed equations (X11).
What changes between the two:
.table 17 20 21
 | Displayed | Inline
Screen | Rows of their own, centered, full size ({c:\displaystyle}) | In the
 line, text size, on the baseline of the text
Print | {c:\[ ... \]}, on its own | {c:$ ... $} in the text
Sums, limits, fractions | Limits above and below, fractions at full size |
 Limits at the side, fractions small
.end
In a table cell or in a box of the printed page an equation is always set
inline, even when it stands alone in the cell; begin the text with
{c:\displaystyle} to get the large form there.  On the screen a table column
that holds only the equation shows it displayed.

.h2 Equations on the screen [eq-screen]
.ix Equation pictures
.ix Equation label
.ix EQN label
.ix latex, dvipng
.ix Equation cache
.ix Equation, rendering
.ix XDG_CACHE_HOME
.ix Cache of equation pictures
The X11 version shows each equation as a picture of the typeset formula.  This
section says what is shown while the picture is not ready or cannot be made,
and what the display modes do.  The terminal version always shows the label.

.h3 How the picture is made [eq-screen-job]
When an equation comes on the screen the Editor makes its picture in the
background, so that typing is not blocked; the picture appears when it is
ready and the text below it moves once.  The pictures have the colors of the
window and a size that follows the font of the window.  A picture that takes
more than 5 seconds is given up and the equation gets the message "{c:latex}
took more than 5 seconds".  An equation with a refused command ({n:eq-refused}) gets the message
"That TeX command is not allowed in an equation", and an empty equation "The
equation is empty".

.h3 The cache [eq-screen-cache]
A picture that was made once is kept, so that the equation is shown at once the
next time the document is opened.  Changing the font of the window or its
colors makes new pictures.  The place where the pictures are kept is in
{x:appcfg-search-cache}; it can be deleted at any time, and the pictures are
made again.

.h3 What is shown meanwhile [eq-screen-label]
Until the picture is ready, and for ever when it cannot be made, the equation
is shown as a label in the color of markers: {c:[EQN text]} with the lines
joined (the name EQN and the text between square brackets).  The label is also
what an equation is in the terminal version.  A failure shows the label and not
the message in the line; the message is in the log of the run ({n:appcfg-log}),
with the block and the line of the equation, and in the message line while the
cursor is on the equation.  {k:Ctrl-W} in the editor of the equation shows it
({n:eq-view}).  A Keycap, a Picture and a LaTeX marker whose pictures cannot be made
are drawn in the Short form in the same way.  An equation that has a command
that does not exist is shown the same way.
.pic pictures/equations-bad.png 5 Ctrl-W on an equation that TeX refused.

.h3 The cursor, movement and wrapping [eq-screen-cursor]
The cursor on an equation is a box round the whole picture.  Up and Down pass
over the rows that only hold a picture as if they were one.  From the text of
an inline equation they go to the line above or below.  A displayed equation
gives two stops: the equation, and the end of its line.  When a line of text
has an inline equation, the line gets taller if the picture is taller than a
row; word wrap moves a picture with the words that come after the break.  A
picture that does not fit in what is left of the line starts the next line and
the text goes on after it.  A picture that is wider than the whole line is
clipped at the right edge.

.h3 Viewing an equation: Ctrl-W [eq-view]
.ix Ctrl-W
.ix latex could not typeset the equation
.ix Typesetting...
In the editor of the equation, {k:Ctrl-W} opens a panel titled Equation over
the editor.  It shows the picture of the text as it is now, three times the
resolution of the picture in the text.  If the picture is larger than the
window it is made again, once, smaller, so that all of it shows.  While the
picture is made the panel shows "Typesetting...".  If TeX could not typeset the
text, the panel shows "latex could not typeset the equation:" and, under it,
the message of TeX with up to three lines after it.  Any key closes the panel,
and {k:Esc} goes back to the editor.  The text is not saved until {k:Ins}.
A picture of an equation that is damaged, has no size, has more than eight
million dots or is wider or taller than 20000 dots is not shown: the panel
gives the reason in the place of the message of TeX, and the label stays in
the text.
.pic pictures/equations-view.png 5 Ctrl-W: the enlarged equation being edited.
Where no picture can be made the panel is a pop-up with a short text:
.table 31 29
Text | When
Viewing an equation needs the X11 version | The terminal version.
{c:latex} and {c:dvipng} are needed to typeset an equation | The X11 version, and {c:latex}
 or {c:dvipng} is not in the system directories.
.end

.h3 Marker display modes [eq-modes]
.ix Marker Display Modes
.ix Display mode, Equation
.ix Inline mode
.ix Global, Marker, Marker Display Modes
The Global menu ({k:F12}, Global, Marker, and then the pages of the Marker Display
Modes with PgUp) has a row Equation that sets how equations are shown.  The
default is Inline.
.pic pictures/equations-modes.png 5 Marker Display Modes: the row Equation.
.table 10 50
Mode | What is shown
Diamond | A diamond glyph in place of the equation.
Name | The name {c:[EQN]}.
Short | {c:[EQN text]}: the name and the text.
Long | {c:[EQUATION text]}.
Hidden | Nothing: the marker takes no room on the line.  It is still in the
 text and is printed.
Inline | The picture (X11 version, {c:latex} and {c:dvipng} found), or {c:[EQN text]}
 when there is no picture yet or none can be made.
.end
Only Inline shows pictures.  The terminal version always draws the labels, and
in the Inline mode it draws {c:[EQN text]}.  A document that has Hidden
equations still prints them.

.h2 Printing equations [eq-print]
.ix Print, equations
.ix Printing equations
.ix Printer, equations
.ix Preview, equations
.ix Long form of an equation
.ix EQUATION label
Final print and Preview (see {n:print}) typeset equations.  A displayed
equation is set on a line of its own; an inline equation, and an equation in a
table cell or a box, is set in the text.  Amsmath and amssymb are loaded for
the whole document; the commands that select a color or define something are
refused ({n:eq-refused}).  The fonts of the text are those that the Global
Typeface settings pick; the math is the math of LaTeX.

.h3 What is refused when printing [eq-print-refuse]
.ix Equation refused
.ix Refused equations
.ix Long form
.ix Printing, refused equation
Before an equation is printed, the text is checked.  An equation that fails is
printed as text instead, in what is called the long form of the marker: the
word EQUATION and the text, in a small typewriter font, in a box.  The rest of
the document is printed as usual and the run does not stop; the Printer
reports the equation as an error ({n:eq-print-fail}).  The checks are these
(the lists are in {n:eq-refused}).
- The text is empty or has only spaces and line ends.
- The text has more than 4000 characters.
- A character above 127 or a control character other than the line end and the
  tab.
- A percent sign ({c:%}), a number sign ({c:#}) or a dollar sign ({c:$}), unless
  written as {c:\%}, {c:\#} and {c:\$}; the character pair {c:^^}.
- A closing brace without an opening one, or braces that do not balance at the
  end.
- A command that is on the list of the refused commands, and any command that
  begins with {c:pdf}, {c:xetex} or {c:luatex}.
- {c:\begin} or {c:\end} whose environment is not on the list of the
  allowed environments, or that has no name in braces.
- Text that TeX itself cannot set as math.
A document therefore always gives a PDF, with the unsuitable equations as text.
The equation reads, for instance,
.code
EQUATION \input{x}
.end
in the box, which is the way to see in the output that something was refused.

.h3 Differences between the screen and the printed page [eq-print-diff]
.ix Screen and print differ
.table 22 18 18
Point | Screen | Print
Checked by | A short list of commands | A longer list, plus the environments
 and the characters
Environments | Anything that TeX takes | Only a list of 13 ({n:eq-refused})
{c:\hspace}, {c:\vspace}, {c:\label} | Accepted by the list | Refused
Percent, number, dollar signs | Reach TeX | Refused unless escaped
Characters above 127 | Reach TeX unchanged | Refused
Length | 1023 characters, the rest is cut | 4000 characters
Colors | Window colors | The colors of the text of the page
Equation alone in a table cell | Displayed | Inline
.end
An equation that you want on both must keep to the common part: the commands
of the tables of this chapter do.  The long form of printing is what you see
if you do not.

.h2 The text of an equation [eq-text]
.ix Math mode
.ix Spaces in equations
.ix Groups
.ix Braces
.ix Special characters in equations
.ix Escape characters
.ix Characters with a meaning in TeX
.ix Letters in equations
.ix Digits in equations
.ix Italics in equations
The text of an equation is read in the math mode of TeX.  The few rules that
the rest of the chapter takes for granted are these.
- Spaces and ends of line are ignored.  {c:a   +   b} and {c:a+b} are the same.
  Spacing is made by the program from the kind of each symbol
  ({n:eq-spacing} says how to change it).
- A letter is a variable and is set in the italic of math ({eq:abc}), a digit is
  upright ({eq:123}), and so is a name that is a command ({eq:\sin x}).  Several
  letters side by side are several variables multiplied, never a word: for a
  word use {c:\text} or {c:\mathrm} ({n:eq-fonts}).
- A command begins with a backslash and a name of letters; it ends at the first
  character that is not a letter.  A space after the name belongs to the name
  and is lost: {c:\alpha x} is {eq:\alpha x}.  A command that is a backslash and
  one non-letter ({c:\, \;} {c:\{{}) has no name to end.
- The braces make a group.  The argument of a command is the next character or
  the next group: {c:\frac12} is {c:\frac{{1}}{{2}}}; {c:x^2} and {c:x^{{2}}}
  are the
  same, but {c:x^{{10}}} needs the braces (without them only the 1 goes up).
  A group alone, {c:{{ }}}, sets what is in it as a unit and makes the sign
  that stands in it an ordinary symbol: {c:a{{+}}b} has no spaces round the
  sign.
- The signs that have a meaning of their own are written as in the table to get
  the character itself.
.ix \ (backslash)
.table 8 23 27
Sign | Meaning in the text | The sign itself
{c:\} | Starts a command | {c:\backslash}
{c:{{} {c:}}} | Group | {c:\{{} and {c:\}}} (or {c:\lbrace \rbrace})
{c:^} | Superscript | {c:\^{{}}} (a hat on nothing); {c:\hat{{}}}; {c:\wedge}
 is a different sign
{c:_} | Subscript | {c:\_}
{c:&} | Separator of the columns of a table | {c:\&}
{c:#} | Parameter of a definition; refused when printed | {c:\#}
{c:%} | Comment; refused when printed | {c:\%}
{c:$} | End of math mode; refused when printed | {c:\$}
{c:~} | A space that does not break (a tie) | {c:\sim} is the sign;
 {c:\textasciitilde} in text
{c:'} | A prime | {c:\prime}, and {c:\textquotesingle} in text
.end
.ix \{{ (brace)
.ix \% (percent)
.ix \# (number sign)
.ix \$ (dollar)
.ix \& (ampersand)
.ix \_ (underscore)
The characters {c:\$ \% \# \& \_ \{{ \}}} are typeset by the commands that the
table gives: {eq:\$ \% \# \& \_ \{{ \}}}.  Characters of the keyboard that have
no
meaning in TeX (the letters, the digits, {c:+ - = < > / ( ) [ ] ! , ; : . ' | @
" `} and
the question mark) are ordinary.  The bar {c:|} is a vertical line, and with
{c:\left} and {c:\right} it grows with its contents; {c:\|} is the double bar.
